# Supply chain cooldown. A version published fewer than this many days ago is not installable here,
# because inside that window "no advisory yet" means detection has not opened, not that a package is
# clean. npm audit being quiet about a package published this morning is not evidence.
#
# This lives in the REPOSITORY and not only in a developer's ~/.npmrc, which is where it was until a
# review pointed out the gap: docs/security-notes.md described a protection that did not exist for
# anyone else who cloned this, including CI, and one of its own dated actions told a reader to run
# `npm install hono@latest` on a machine where nothing would hold that install back. A security
# property that holds on one laptop is not a property of the project.
#
# Measured before committing, because a cooldown that broke every CI run would be a worse problem
# than the one it solves: with this set to 30, `npm ci` on a tree with no node_modules still
# installed the locked versions and exited 0. `npm ci` reads the lockfile rather than resolving, so
# the cooldown governs `npm install` and leaves reproducible installs alone.
#
# It is never bypassed to clear an advisory faster. When a fix is published inside the window, the
# answer is an entry in scripts/accepted-advisories.json with a short recheck date.
min-release-age=3
